<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gebre Mesqel Lalibela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 12th–early 13th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gebre Mesqel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> who reigned in the late 12th and early 13th centuries. Tradition credits him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> at his capital Roha, conceived as a 'New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>', and the town was later renamed Lalibela after him. He is venerated as a saint by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebre-mesqel-lalibela.jpg" alt="Gebre Mesqel Lalibela" width="280" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown artist, originally made in Lalibela area, Ethiopia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebre_Mesqel_Lalibela.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
